{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Work, Power and Energy","count":11,"questions":[{"id":52579,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Work, Power and Energy","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A uniform thin rod of length $2 \\mathrm{~L}$ and mass $\\mathrm{m}$ lies on a horizontal table. A horizontal inpulse $\\mathrm{J}$ is given to the rod at one end. There is no friction. The total K.E. of the rod just after the impulse will be :","options":["$\\mathrm{J}^{2} / 2 \\mathrm{~m}$","$\\mathrm{J}^{2} / \\mathrm{m}$","$2 \\mathrm{~J}^{2} / \\mathrm{m}$","$6 \\mathrm{~J}^{2} / \\mathrm{m}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Ftj9BFSLSeinW5n6Q1BHn4zoWddVvthWSeTLnK6Sphu8.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Ftj9BFSLSeinW5n6Q1BHn4zoWddVvthWSeTLnK6Sphu8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Ftj9BFSLSeinW5n6Q1BHn4zoWddVvthWSeTLnK6Sphu8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$\\mathrm{J}=\\mathrm{mv} \\quad \\mathrm{J} . \\mathrm{L}=\\frac{\\mathrm{M}(2 \\mathrm{~L})^{2}}{12} \\omegav^{v}=\\mathrm{J} / \\mathrm{M}\\mathrm{Cm}J . L=\\frac{M L^{2}}{3} \\omega\\omega=\\frac{3 J}{M L}\\mathrm{ke}=\\frac{\\mathrm{J}^{2}}{2 \\mathrm{~m}}+\\frac{3 \\mathrm{~J}^{2}}{2 \\mathrm{~m}}=2 \\mathrm{~J}^{2} / \\mathrm{m}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2134ff5b3fc542244b7"},{"id":52580,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Work, Power and Energy","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Water falls down a $500.0 \\mathrm{~m}$ shaft to reach a turbine which generates electricity. How much water must fall per second in order to generate $1.00 \\times 10^{9}$ Watts of power? (Assume $50 \\%$ efficiency of conversion and $\\left.\\mathrm{g}=10 \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s}^{2}\\right)$","options":["$250 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}$","$400 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}$","$500 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}$","$200 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$500 \\mathrm{~m}\\left[\\begin{array}{c}\\mathrm{P}=\\frac{\\mathrm{mah}}{\\operatorname{time}} \\\\ \\eta=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}_{\\text {cut }}}{\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{input}}} \\\\ \\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{cut}}}{\\eta}\\end{array}\\right.=\\frac{10^{9}}{0.5}P_{\\text {in }}=2 \\times 10^{9}\\frac{\\text { mah }}{\\text { time }}=2 \\times 10^{9}\\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{t}=\\frac{2 \\times 10^{9}}{10 \\times 500}=\\frac{2}{5} \\times 10^{6}=4 \\times 10^{5}=400 \\mathrm{~m}^{3}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d2131301d1734fef471f"},{"id":52581,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Work, Power and Energy","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Uniform ring of radius $\\mathrm{R}$ is moving on a horizontal surface with speed $\\mathrm{v}$ and then climbs up a ramp of inclination $30^{\\circ}$ to a hcight $h$. Thcre is no slipping in the cntirc motion. Then h is","options":["$\\mathrm{v}^{2} / 2 \\mathrm{~g}$","$\\mathrm{v}^{2} / \\mathrm{g}$","$3 \\mathrm{v}^{2} / 2 \\mathrm{~g}$","$2 \\mathrm{v}^{2} / \\mathrm{g}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"\\{Using conservation of energy)\n\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{mV}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{mR}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{V}}{\\mathrm{R}}\\right)^{2}=\\mathrm{mgh} \\\\\n\\mathrm{m}\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{V}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\mathrm{V}^{2}}{2}\\right)=\\mathrm{mgh} \\\\\n\\mathrm{h}=\\frac{\\mathrm{V}^{2}}{\\mathrm{~g}}\n\\end{array}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213365d98e1841703b3"},{"id":52582,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Work, Power and Energy","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A particle slides from the top of a smooth hemispherical surface of radius R which is fixed on a horizontal surface. If it separates from the hemisphere at a height h from the horizontal surface, then the speed of the particle is","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo></msqrt></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo> </mo></mfrac></msqrt></math>","<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>R</mi></msqrt></math></p>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></msqrt></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>Condition given in question is shown below.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fa2048dca-f0e1-4a28-ac22-171fef8c674a-image-20201224172741-1.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fa2048dca-f0e1-4a28-ac22-171fef8c674a-image-20201224172741-1.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fa2048dca-f0e1-4a28-ac22-171fef8c674a-image-20201224172741-1.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo></math> speed of particle when it separates from hemisphere. As there is no friction, loss of potential energy appears in form of kinetic energy of particle.</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo> </mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mroot><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></mroot></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SA)","_marksId":"614da48d7d50583290e86390"},{"id":52583,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Work, Power and Energy","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A proton of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> and charge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi></math> is projected from a very large distance towards an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math> -particle with velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo></math> Initially <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math> -particle is at rest, but it is free to move. If gravity is neglected, then the minimum separation along the straight line of their motion will be<br />\n ","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo></mrow></msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo></mrow></msub><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math> -particle is free to move, initial kinetic energy of system will be</p>\n\n<p>initial kinetic energy of system will be</p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>μ</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>\n<p>where, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo></math> reduced mass of system</p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>·</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>\n<p>Now, by energy conservation, we have<br />\nInitial kinetic energy = Potential energy at minimum separation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math></p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>·</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mfenced><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>·</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac></math>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mo> </mo><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo> </mo><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da40a4b91472175a15874"},{"id":52584,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Work, Power and Energy","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>A wide bottom cylindrical massless plastic container of height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn></math> identical coins inside it and is floating on water with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> inside the water. If we start putting more of such coins on its lid, it is observed that after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> coins are put, its equilibrium changes from stable to unstable. Equilibrium in floating is stable if the geometric center of the submerged portion is above the center of mass of the object). The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> is closest to</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F5a6e507c-da36-49f3-95f6-4adbf52c4aab-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F5a6e507c-da36-49f3-95f6-4adbf52c4aab-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F5a6e507c-da36-49f3-95f6-4adbf52c4aab-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>24</mn></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>Let mass of each coin be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>.</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> Location of center of mass after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> coins are kept on lid from bottom of container is</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>Nm</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mrow><mfenced><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Also height of submerged portion after keeping <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> coins on lid will be, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow><mn>40</mn></mfrac><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> Equilibrium will just be stable if </p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>40</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mrow><mfenced><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mrow></mfenced></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>480</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4800</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>⇒</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>72</mn></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SA)","_marksId":"614da50bdaf788efbe9c5e1e"},{"id":52585,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Work, Power and Energy","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A proton and an antiproton come close to each other in vacuum such that the distance between them is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>.</mo></math> Consider the potential energy to be zero at infinity. The velocity at this distance will be:","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>17</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>23</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>/</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>Applying mechanical energy conservation<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mfenced><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfenced><mfenced><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced></mrow><mi>r</mi></mfrac></math><br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced 