mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo><</mo><mn>0</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>></mo><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo></math>. The schematic variation of oscillation period <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math> for a particle performing periodic motion in this potential as a function of its energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi></math> is","options":["<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fe7e11adf-b079-4e2b-9bb0-7f4694beaf17-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fe7e11adf-b079-4e2b-9bb0-7f4694beaf17-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fe7e11adf-b079-4e2b-9bb0-7f4694beaf17-image.png?alt=media\"/>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Febaeb3df-b8d2-44d4-b580-ffdeb4367f7e-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Febaeb3df-b8d2-44d4-b580-ffdeb4367f7e-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Febaeb3df-b8d2-44d4-b580-ffdeb4367f7e-image.png?alt=media\"/>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F6156e5e9-1864-485a-9108-de8359456d0c-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F6156e5e9-1864-485a-9108-de8359456d0c-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F6156e5e9-1864-485a-9108-de8359456d0c-image.png?alt=media\"/>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F6f642b09-5e0b-42f3-a3e1-7ee1584d9d43-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F6f642b09-5e0b-42f3-a3e1-7ee1584d9d43-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F6f642b09-5e0b-42f3-a3e1-7ee1584d9d43-image.png?alt=media\"/>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p> Given, potential function for the oscillating particle is</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"\" open=\"{\"><mtable columnalign=\"left\"><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>x</mi><mo><</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>x</mi><mo>></mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p>So, potential energy of the particle (mass<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>) is</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>U</mi><mfenced><mi>x</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"\" open=\"{\"><mtable columnalign=\"left\"><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>x</mi><mo><</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>x</mi><mo><</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>U</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"\" open=\"{\"><mtable columnalign=\"left\"><mtr><mtd><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>x</mi><mo><</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>x</mi><mo>></mo><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p>If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo> </mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>U</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mi>a</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Now, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>U</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>></mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>So, particle is in unstable equilibrium at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mi>a</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Hence, particle is unbounded for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>></mo><mi>x</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>></mo><mi>a</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>In region, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></math>, time period of particle reduces from a maximum.<br />\nSo, correct graph is (b)..</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da40a383d7d74e8d3c195"},{"id":52587,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Work, Power and Energy","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A particle with total mechanical energy, which is small and negative, is under the influence of a one dimensional potential $\\mathrm{U}(\\mathrm{x})=\\mathrm{x}^{4} / 4-\\mathrm{x}^{2} / 2 \\mathrm{~J}$ Where $\\mathrm{x}$ is in meters. At time $\\mathrm{t}=0 \\mathrm{~s}$, it is at $\\mathrm{x}=-0.5 \\mathrm{~m}$. Then at a later time it can be found","options":["Anywhere on the x axis","Between $\\mathrm{x}=-1.0 \\mathrm{~m}$ to $\\mathrm{x}=1.0 \\mathrm{~m}$","Between $\\mathrm{x}=-1.0 \\mathrm{~m}$ to $\\mathrm{x}=0.0 \\mathrm{~m}$","Between $\\mathrm{x}=0.0 \\mathrm{~m}$ to $\\mathrm{x}=1.0 \\mathrm{~m}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"at $\\mathrm{t}=0, \\quad \\mathrm{x}=0.5$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &=\\frac{x^{4}}{4}-\\frac{x^{2}}{2} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\Rightarrow\\left|\\frac{1}{4}\\right| \\\\\n\\frac{d u}{d x} &=\\frac{4 x^{3}}{4}-\\frac{2 x}{2}=x^{3}-x \\\\\n\\frac{d u}{d x} &=x\\left(x^{2}-1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n\n$\\frac{\\mathrm{du}}{\\mathrm{dx}}=0$ at point of maxima \\& minima $\\mathrm{x}=0 ; \\mathrm{x}=\\pm 1\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^{2} \\mathrm{u}}{\\mathrm{dx}^{2}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{x}=0}=-1$ point of maxima\n$\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^{2} \\mathrm{u}}{\\mathrm{dx}^{2}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{x}=\\pm 1}=2$ point of minima\n$<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FLUDZj5Use6XN7C5e6SHcpwMu9fdfSKPKvf8TM9Z46JI.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FLUDZj5Use6XN7C5e6SHcpwMu9fdfSKPKvf8TM9Z46JI.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FLUDZj5Use6XN7C5e6SHcpwMu9fdfSKPKvf8TM9Z46JI.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">$ \\\\ \nparticle will found between $(-1,0)$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213dbb0df289238b046"},{"id":52588,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Work, Power and Energy","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A block of mass $\\mathrm{m}$ is sliding down an inclined plane with constant speed. At a certain instant $\\mathrm{t}_{0}$, its height above the ground is $\\mathrm{h}$. The coefficient of kinetic friction between the block and the plane is $\\mu$. If the block reaches the ground at a later instant $\\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{g}}$, then the energy dissipated by friction in the time interval $\\left(\\mathrm{t}_{\\mathrm{g}}-\\mathrm{t}_{0}\\right)$ is-\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FPMmrEt7-8XZxtSYX5he4jBUkeKQYLEXYlUhdKn3sb6s.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FPMmrEt7-8XZxtSYX5he4jBUkeKQYLEXYlUhdKn3sb6s.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FPMmrEt7-8XZxtSYX5he4jBUkeKQYLEXYlUhdKn3sb6s.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$\\mu \\mathrm{mgh}$","$\\mu \\mathrm{mgh} / \\sin \\theta$","$\\mathrm{mgh}$ )","$\\mu \\mathrm{mgh} / \\cos \\theta$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{f}}+\\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{mg}}=\\Delta \\mathrm{K} . \\mathrm{E} .,(\\Delta \\mathrm{K} . \\mathrm{E} \\cdot=0) \\\\\n\\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{f}}=-\\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{mg}} \\\\\n\\mathrm{W}_{\\mathrm{f}}=-\\mathrm{mgh} \\\\\n\\therefore \\text { Energy dissipated }=\\mathrm{mgh}\n\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213336e39c7cbb095db"},{"id":52589,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Work, Power and Energy","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A block of mass $m$ slides from rest at a height $\\mathrm{H}$ on a frictionless inclined plane as shown in the figure. It travels a distance d across a rough horizontal surface with coefficient of kinetic friction $\\mu$, and compresses a spring of spring $\\mathrm{k}$ by a distance $\\mathrm{x}$ before coming to rest momentarily. Then the spring extends and the block travels back attaining a final height of $h$. Then\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F7SH5hw9d23YqA6S5wuW_rnDU8Fz-_vrt0nFvFXPdhkM.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F7SH5hw9d23YqA6S5wuW_rnDU8Fz-_vrt0nFvFXPdhkM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F7SH5hw9d23YqA6S5wuW_rnDU8Fz-_vrt0nFvFXPdhkM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["$h=H-2 \\mu(d+x)$","$h=H+2 \\mu(d+x)$","$h=\\mathrm{H}-2 \\mu \\mathrm{d}+\\mathrm{kx}^{2} / \\mathrm{mg}$","$=H-2 \\mu(d+x)+k x^{2} / 2 m g$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\mathrm{mgH}-2 \\mu \\mathrm{mg}(\\mathrm{d}+\\mathrm{x})-\\mathrm{mgh}=0\\mathrm{~h}=\\mathrm{H}-2 \\mu(\\mathrm{d}+\\mathrm{x})$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213d1544164629426e1"}]}